            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "southeastern tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The EOF mode 1 of SLP, explaining 21.7% of the total variance, shows an east-west separated pattern in the vector loading (Fig. 1d). The maximum negative loadings situated over the western tropical Pacific to the eastern Indian Ocean off west Australia are in contrast to the maximum positive loadings in the southeastern tropical Pacific, establishing a typical pattern of the Walker circulation. In gross aspect, it is very similar to the oscillating behavior of El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) when combined with the PC time series (Philander 1989).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "central Pacific (CP) El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "central Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This type of El Ni\u00f1o, referred to as central Pacific (CP) El Ni\u00f1o in this study, is characterized by anomalous warming in the central Pacific, in contrast to the conventional El Ni\u00f1o that shows the largest warming in the eastern Pacific and which is referred to as eastern Pacific (EP) El Ni\u00f1o in this study.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "eastern Pacific (EP) El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This type of El Ni\u00f1o, referred to as central Pacific (CP) El Ni\u00f1o in this study, is characterized by anomalous warming in the central Pacific, in contrast to the conventional El Ni\u00f1o that shows the largest warming in the eastern Pacific and which is referred to as eastern Pacific (EP) El Ni\u00f1o in this study.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "central tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The stronger boundary-layer effective easterlies over the central tropical Pacific are consistent with the strengthened east-west SLP gradient over the tropical Pacific (and thus strengthened Walker circulation).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific basin",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We considered the intensified Walker circulation in recent decades as a working hypothesis. Variations in meteorological or surface variables are expected to agree with the intensified circulation, i.e., increased deep convective activities and associated upper tropospheric moistening over the western Pacific ascending region and increased SLP over the eastern Pacific descending region in contrast to decreased SLP over the western Pacific ascending region; an enhanced easterly wind is expected in the boundary layer over the entire tropical Pacific basin in response to the increased east-west SLP gradient.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CP El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As a cause of the intensified Walker circulation, we proposed the frequent occurrences of CP El Ni\u00f1o in the most recent decade, as a result of which the eastern tropical Pacific and western Pacific have undergone cooling and warming, respectively, over the three decades.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CP El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "western Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As a cause of the intensified Walker circulation, we proposed the frequent occurrences of CP El Ni\u00f1o in the most recent decade, as a result of which the eastern tropical Pacific and western Pacific have undergone cooling and warming, respectively, over the three decades.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Apart from these results, other mechanisms that could explain the increased Walker circulation exist. For example, the mean background state change over the three decades might be connected to the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO; Parker et al. 2007) or Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO; Mantuna et al. 1997), which is the most dominant SST variability in the Pacific Ocean on the low-frequency timescales.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Apart from these results, other mechanisms that could explain the increased Walker circulation exist. For example, the mean background state change over the three decades might be connected to the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation (IPO; Parker et al. 2007) or Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO; Mantuna et al. 1997), which is the most dominant SST variability in the Pacific Ocean on the low-frequency timescales.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In addition, it may be possible that the enhanced Walker circulation leads to a strong atmospheric stability over the eastern tropical Pacific due to the stronger sinking motion, which brings in weak ocean-atmospheric feedback processes there.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
